+// ── THE FOCUS RING'S GEOMETRY (T9-W7 §5) ──────────────────────────────────────
+//
+// Geometry, not time and not a tuner knob: frozen, beside BOIL_CONFIG rather than in it.
+// (`BoilConfig` is reactive and every field of it is a slider the tuner can drag; these
+// three live inside the `useBoilCache(["cellRects", …])` key's closure, where a mutation
+// could never reach a dealt board anyway.)
+//
+// The library's law is proportional — `maxDisplace = roughness × len × 0.015`
+// (pencil-boil `wobbleLinePoints`) — so at one roughness a 53.75-unit cell edge wanders
+// 15× less than a 948-unit rule and the ring reads as CAD. Naming the wander itself and
+// solving the roughness back out makes it size-free BY CONSTRUCTION, exactly:
+//
+//     roughness(cell) = wanderUnits / (0.015 × inset × cellSize)
+//     maxDisplace     = roughness × (inset × cellSize) × 0.015 = wanderUnits    ← exact
+//
+// σ is a statistic of a WINDOW, so the window is declared with the number: W4 = all four
+// edges, the middle 80% of each edge's own arc, residual to that edge's NOMINAL line,
+// pooled. On W4 the ring reads 2.392 units ±5% at 4×4 / 9×9 / 16×16 with the product's own
+// seeds, against a 4096-cell population of the same primitive — one number, three boards.
+// The grid rule's own W4 σ, re-taken in the SAME run over every rule the board draws, puts
+// the ring-to-rule ratio near 0.66 on screen (the ghost renders at boardPx/1300, the grid
+// at boardPx/1000). Every ratio this family quotes comes from that one run, never from a
+// transposed single row; the shape constant that predicts σ from `wanderUnits` lives with
+// the gate that checks it, not here — the product never read it.
+//
+// σ in the reader's px at 1280×800: 0.76 / 1.02 / 1.03 px at 4×4 / 9×9 / 16×16, which is
+// 1.1% / 2.5% / 4.5% of the drawn edge. Constant in UNITS, so the reader sees it 4× wider at
+// 16×16 than at 4×4 — a hand's tremor is a length, not a fraction, and the grid's own rules
+// carry the same absolute wander.
+//
+// WHAT THE INSET BUYS. Not separation from the rule: the rule wobbles too, and the frame runs
+// 12 u inside every edge cell by the grid's design, so the ring shares ink with a rule on the
+// worst cells at 16×16 whatever the inset. That clearance is a READING the probe banks (MA-R),
+// never a gate. The law the ring is held to is MA-N (below) and legibility on whatever it
+// crosses.
+//
+// MA-N, what the inset IS load-bearing for — the ring stays inside its own square at the
+// heaviest tier the sheet draws (stroke 10, invalid AND selected), 16×16, every cell:
+//     f = 1.00 → the ring's outer ink ENTERS the neighbour by 0.788 u = 0.44 px
+//     f = 0.86 → it clears by 2.577 u = 1.43 px
+// (tier 2, stroke 7: f = 1 gives +0.366 u, a hair; f = 0.86 gives +3.731 u.) The inset pays
+// for the absolute wander: the old ring (roughness 0.4, no inset) wandered 0.375 u at 16×16
+// and cleared on its own. 0.90 is the banked alternate for the owner's re-look (+1.616 u).
+export const RING_GEOMETRY = Object.freeze({
+  /** the ring's wander in ghost units — `maxDisplace`, one number for every board */
+  wanderUnits: 5.4,
+  /** the ring is drawn at this fraction of the cell, centred: a mark INSIDE the square */
+  inset: 0.86,
+});
